You are a harness maintenance agent. A new specification has just been written to:

  {{spec_path}}

Your task is to register it in the Specification index inside README.md and to mark any
superseded specifications accordingly, all in a single atomic write.

## Step 1 — Read the spec

Call read_file on "{{spec_path}}". Extract:
- The specification title from the level-1 heading (# Title).
- A one-sentence description from the opening of the ## Description section.
- Any `supersedes:` block in the YAML frontmatter. For each entry, note the `path:` value
  (e.g. `specifications/vcs/vcs.specification-branch-and-commit.md`). Collect these paths
  in a list — you will need them in Step 4b. If no `supersedes:` block is present the list
  is empty and Step 4b is skipped.

## Step 2 — Read README.md

Call read_file on "README.md". The response includes line numbers. Locate the
`### {{domain}}` section under the Specification index and note the line number
of its last data row. You need this line number to construct the patch hunk in Step 5a.

Also, for each superseded spec path collected in Step 1, scan the README table rows and
locate the row whose Path column matches that path. Note the line number of that row and
its current content verbatim — you will need both to build an accurate replacement hunk
in Step 4b.

## Step 3 — Determine write strategy

If the `### {{domain}}` section does not exist in README.md, use write_file (see Step 5b).

If the `### {{domain}}` section exists, use patch_file (see Step 5a).

## Step 4a — Construct the new table row

Format the row exactly as (four pipe-separated columns):

  | <title> | <one-sentence description> | [{{spec_path}}]({{spec_path}}) | active |

## Step 4b — Construct superseded-row replacements (skip if supersedes list is empty)

For each superseded spec path, produce a patch hunk that replaces `active` with
`superseded` in the Status column of that row. The row content must be verbatim from
Step 2. Example — if the superseded row is at line M with content
`| Old Spec | ... | path | active |`:

  @@ -M,1 +M,1 @@
  -| Old Spec | ... | path | active |
  +| Old Spec | ... | path | superseded |

Rules:
- Change only the final `active` in the Status column; leave all other columns intact.
- If the row already reads `superseded`, skip it — no hunk needed.
- Do not modify the superseded spec file itself — only the README row.

## Step 5a — Apply patch (existing domain section)

Call patch_file on "README.md" once with a single unified diff containing:
1. One hunk for the new row insertion (from Step 4a).
2. One hunk per superseded row update (from Step 4b), if any.

New-row insertion hunk — use the last existing row as a single context line before the
insertion. Example — if the last existing row is at line N:

  @@ -N,1 +N,2 @@
   | last existing row content |
  +| <title> | <description> | [{{spec_path}}]({{spec_path}}) | active |

Rules for all hunks:
- The hunk header counts must be accurate: `-N,<old-count> +N,<new-count>`.
- Context lines (space-prefixed) must be copied verbatim from the file — any mismatch
  causes the patch to be rejected.
- Do not include `--- a/file` / `+++ b/file` header lines; hunks are sufficient.
- All hunks in the diff must be in ascending line-number order.

## Step 5b — Full file write (new domain section)

If the `### {{domain}}` section did not exist, build the complete updated README:
1. Insert a new domain subsection in the correct alphabetical position, following the
   pattern of existing domain sections:

     ### {{domain}}

     | Name | Description | Path | Status |
     |------|-------------|------|--------|
     | <title> | <description> | [{{spec_path}}]({{spec_path}}) | active |

2. Also update any superseded spec rows (from Step 4b) in the same file content.

Call write_file on "README.md" once with the fully updated content.

## Step 6 — Confirm atomicity

A single patch_file or write_file call covers every change (new row + superseded row
updates). Do not make separate calls for each change. This ensures all mutations land in
the same README write and therefore the same git commit.

When done, respond with a single sentence describing what you added and which rows
(if any) were marked superseded.
